  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 AM EDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nA weak shortwave will move into the Ohio valley during the day \nMonday and then push east of the forecast area by Tuesday morning, \nall of which is ahead of a closed upper low shifting east through \nthe northern plains and into the Upper Midwest. At the surface, this \nwave will push a weak cold front towards the Tennessee valley during \nthe day, lingering just to our west and northwest Monday night \nthrough Tuesday. As such, expect this to be a focus for increased \nconvective coverage both Monday and Tuesday. Forecast soundings show \nenough instability to be concerned about a few strong to severe \nstorms on Monday capable of damaging winds. The overall threat isn't \nhigh, but the combination of decent instability along with high \nPWATs means that a few damaging wind gusts similar to what was seen \na day or two ago could occur. Additionally, with slow storm motions, \nsome isolated flash flooding could occur. Tuesday will be similar in \ncoverage as the Ohio valley upper low begins to shift southeast, but \ninstability will be notably less so outside of the far southern \nparts of the area, think that any severe threat is low.\n\nRegardless of the expected storms for Monday and Tuesday, the heat \nwill continue. Highs in the 80s and 90s are expected.\n\nOn Wednesday the pattern change gets underway and we begin to see a \nbreak in the relentless heat. By Wednesday a rex block pattern takes \nshape as an upper ridge builds over the Canadian side of the Great \nLakes region, and the aforementioned Ohio valley upper low deepens \nover the Ohio valley and central Appalachian region. With the upper \nlow to our north and NW flow aloft, this will push the cold front \nthrough the southern Appalachians and allow the cooldown to begin. \nBy Thursday through the weekend we should see highs topping out only \nin the 70's for every except southeast TN where they may squeak \nacross the 80 degree mark. This pattern will also be fairly dry with \na more Canadian airmass moving in.",
